When running multiple Next.js instances, tag invalidation must be coordinated across instances. The default in-memory handler only tracks tags locally, so calling revalidateTag() on one instance does not affect others.

To coordinate tags across instances:

updateTags() is called when revalidateTag() is invoked. Your handler should write the invalidation timestamp to shared storage.refreshTags() is called before each request. Your handler should read recent invalidation events from shared storage and update its local tag state.getExpiration() returns the most recent revalidation timestamp across all provided tags. The default implementation returns Math.max(...timestamps, 0).

